The solution correctly rewrites n² + 1 = (n+5)(n−5) + 24, so n+5 must divide 24. With n ≥ 1, n+5 ≥ 6, giving n+5 ∈ {6, 8, 12, 24}, i.e., n ∈ {1, 3, 7, 19}. Verify: 6|2 ✓, 8|10 ✓, 12|50 ✓, 24|362 ✓ (362/24 = 15.08... wait, check: 19²+1=362, 362/24 is not an integer? 24·15=360, remainder 2. Hmm, but 24 divides 24, and n²+1 = (24)(14)+24 = 336+24 = 360 ≠ 362. Actually n−5 = 14, (n+5)(n−5)=360, +24 = 384? No: n²=361, 361−25=336, 336+24=361? Wait, n²−25=336, plus 24 = 360, but n²+1 = 362. Error in arithmetic: n²−25+24 = n²−1, not n²+1!

Let me redo: n²+1 = (n²−25) + 26 = (n+5)(n−5) + 26. So n+5 must divide 26: divisors ≥6 are 13 and 26 → n = 8, 21. Sum = 29. Check: 8²+1=65=13·5 ✓; 21²+1=442=26·17 ✓.

